lone"}],"gloss":"he alone","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"ஆகிப்","parts":[{"text":"ஆகிப்","lemma":"ஆகிப்","gloss":"having become"}],"gloss":"having become","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"பொருது","parts":[{"text":"பொருது","lemma":"பொருதல்","gloss":"having fought"}],"gloss":"having fought"},{"surface":"களத்து","parts":[{"text":"களத்து","lemma":"களத்து","gloss":"on the battlefield"}],"gloss":"on the battlefield","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"அடலே","parts":[{"text":"அடல்","lemma":"அடல்","gloss":"conquering/killing"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ஏ","gloss":"indeed"}],"gloss":"conquering indeed"}]}],"literal":"One person conquering another and being defeated / is not a new thing, it is the nature of this world. / But before today, we have not heard of this: / wearing the bright tender leaves of the big branch of the neem tree with a round trunk in the common ground, / woven closely with the long creeper of the Uzhiñai plant, / a tightly strung honey-dripping chaplet, / along with a thick garland, so as to shine splendidly, / while the clear-sounding drum of sweet sound reverberates, beautiful to behold, / Chezhiyan of golden ornaments, possessing a country of great wealth, / whose greatness and pride they did not know, those who gathered / saying 'we will fight' and came against him, / the seven kings wearing adorned hero's anklets, subduing their great strength, / he alone, fighting on the battlefield, conquering them!","translation":"For one man to conquer another, or to be defeated, is nothing new; it is the ancient way of this world. But never before today have we heard of such a thing: wearing a tightly woven, honey-dripping chaplet made of the bright tender leaves from the large branch of the round-trunked neem tree in the public square, intertwined with long creepers of the balloon vine, shining splendidly alongside a thick garland, while the clear-toned drums of sweet sound reverberate in a beautiful spectacle, Chezhiyan of golden ornaments, ruler of a wealthy land, stood alone. The seven kings who wore adorned warrior anklets, ignorant of his greatness and pride, had gathered together saying, 'We shall fight him,' and marched against him. Yet, standing all by himself, he fought on the battlefield and completely subdued their great strength!","modern":"ஒருவன் மற்றொருவனை வெல்வதும் தோற்பதும் இந்த உலகத்திற்குப் புதியது அல்ல; அது உலக இயற்கைதான். ஆனால், இன்றைய நாளுக்கு முன்னால் நாம் கேள்விப்படாத ஒன்று நிகழ்ந்துள்ளது. மன்றத்தில் உள்ள திரண்ட அடிமரத்தைக் கொண்ட வேப்ப மரத்தின் பெரிய கிளையிலிருந்து பறித்த ஒளிரும் தளிர் இலைகளையும், நீண்ட உழிஞைக் கொடிகளையும் சேர்த்து நெருக்கமாகத் தொடுத்த, தேன் சிந்தும் தலையாலங்கானத்துக் கண்ணியை, அடர்ந்த மாலையோடு சேர்த்துப் பொலிவுறச் சூடிக்கொண்டு, இனிய ஓசையுடைய தெளிவான கிணைப்பறை முழங்க, காண்பவர் வியக்கும்படி, வளமிக்க நாட்டை உடையவனும் பொன்னணிகளை அணிந்தவனுமாகிய நெடுஞ்செழியன் நின்றான். அவனது பெருமையையும் ஆற்றலையும் அறியாமல் ஒன்று கூடி, 'அவனோடு போர் செய்வோம்' என்று அவனிடத்தே வந்த, வீரக்கழல் அணிந்த ஏழு அரசர்களின் வலிமையெல்லாம் அழியும்படி, அவன் ஒருவனாகவே நின்று போர்க்களத்தில் அவர்களை வென்றான்.","explanation":"The poet Idaikkunrur Kizhar is praising the young Pandya king, Nedunchezhiyan, after his historic victory at the battle of Talaiyalanganam. The poet begins by stating that victory and defeat are common in warfare and have been the way of the world since time immemorial. However, what is unprecedented is a single young king standing alone against a coalition of seven powerful enemy kings and defeating them all. The imagery of the king wearing a chaplet woven from the neem leaves (the emblem of the Pandyas) and the Uzhiñai creeper (symbolizing siege and victory) highlights his readiness for battle. The sounding of the clear-toned drums adds to the heroic atmosphere of the battlefield. The key words 'வேம்பு' (neem) represents the Pandya lineage, 'உழிஞை' (balloon vine) represents the theme of warfare, and 'எழுவர்' (the seven kings) highlights the scale of his victory.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"},"t